\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}
\paragraph{Tasks.}
All four tasks have a program verifier, so rewards are exact and no reward model is involved (details in Appendix~\ref{app:datasets}). \textbf{Countdown} \citep{gandhi2024sos}: reach a target from four to five numbers with $+,-,\times,\div$; the answer is a derivation. \textbf{Sudoku} \citep{shah2024sudoku}: fill a $9\times9$ grid; solutions are unique; the model emits all 81 cells. \textbf{Knights-and-Knaves} (K\&K) \citep{xie2024kk}: assign knight/knave to $n\in\{4,5,6\}$ characters from their statements; solutions are unique. \textbf{Game24} \citep{yao2023tot}: reach 24 from four numbers. Game24 has only 1{,}345 solvable number sets, so we split by \emph{set}: the 1{,}601 evaluation prompts are orderings of 269 sets the model has never seen, and on this split more supervised fine-tuning \emph{hurts} (Appendix~\ref{app:g24_sft}), so no RL gain can be attributed to continued SFT.

\paragraph{Models and protocol.}
Every run starts from ELF-B (105M) fine-tuned on the task with the ELF recipe, at a checkpoint with headroom (Countdown epoch 60; Sudoku the converged model; K\&K epoch 4, since epoch 8 already reaches 99.7 and serves as the supervised oracle; Game24 the epoch-20 peak). We report accuracy under four samplers, ODE64 / $\gamma4$@16 / $\gamma28$@32 / $\gamma56$@64 ($\gamma14$@16 replaces $\gamma28$@32 for Sudoku), on 2{,}000 held-out prompts (1{,}601 for Game24). GRPO is the score-function baseline (group size 8, uniform step credit, no KL, best of six checkpoints), the strongest member of the family in Table~\ref{tab:sf_variants}; \jpc{} uses $10^{-6}$ with the 2D critic and the two-phase $3\times10^{-6}\!\to\!10^{-6}$ schedule with the scalar critic, with the held-out peak reported (Appendix~\ref{app:details}). Every headline number is the mean $\pm$ standard deviation over three seeds of the per-seed peak (evaluation jitter $\approx\pm0.3$).

