\paragraph{Pure RL.}
Table~\ref{tab:main} gives the headline numbers (Figure~\ref{fig:results} in Appendix~\ref{app:results} plots them with seed error bars). On \textbf{Game24}, where the test set is unseen number sets and supervised training cannot help, \jpc{} with the 2D critic reaches $58.7\pm1.6$ ($56.8/60.6/58.8$ over seeds) against $45.0$ for the base, $47.0$ for GRPO and $32.2$ for the AR control; the gain of $+13.7$ points is the largest we observed and it replicates on every seed. On \textbf{Countdown} the scalar two-phase schedule has the higher ceiling when it works ($+9.0$ and $+7.0$; the third seed never learns), the 2D critic gives $+5.6$ on both seeds, and the loop reaches $54.4$. On \textbf{Sudoku} and \textbf{K\&K} the two estimators land in the same band at the deployment sampler ($+3$ to $+4$) with very different reliability (the 2D critic's seeds agree within $0.4$ points, GRPO's spread over up to $7$, one Sudoku seed ending below the base), and they differ most at the cheap samplers, which \jpc{} lifts by $+5$ to $+8$ while GRPO leaves them near the base (Appendix~\ref{app:results}).

\paragraph{The AR control.}
The score-function estimator is not the problem in itself. A GPT-2 fine-tuned on the same Countdown data climbs from 44.2 to 53.9 under token-level GRPO/PPO with textbook clip statistics, while the same estimator on the flow model, with every advantage refinement of Table~\ref{tab:sf_variants}, stays within $+5$. On the Game24 set split the AR model gains $+5.3$ from a lower start and the flow model $+13.7$ through its native estimator: each architecture improves through its own gradient channel, and the continuous one has the larger channel. Appendix~\ref{app:d1} runs d1 \citep{zhao2025d1}, a masked-diffusion baseline, on our tasks.

\paragraph{The loop and its controls.}
Figure~\ref{fig:curves} (right) isolates the loop on Game24. Four rounds from the RL policy take the deployment sampler from $55.2$ to $58.3$ and the 64-step ODE sampler from $41.2$ to $52.7$ ($+17.5$ over the base), so a sampler four times cheaper than the deployment one ends within six points of it. Two controls hold the problems fixed: supervised fine-tuning on the \emph{gold} derivations of the very orderings the loop sampled gives $55.5\!\to\!53.8$, nothing; the identical loop from the SFT base gives $+0.8$ after one round and $+3.1$ after two, against $+11.4$ after one round from the RL policy (Countdown: $+1.9$ against $+12.2$, Appendix~\ref{app:cd_loop}). The gain is therefore in the RL policy's derivations: on-distribution, diverse (up to four verified derivations per problem), and, unlike gold labels, things the policy can already almost do.

\paragraph{Where it does not help.}
Sudoku's loop is null, on training puzzles and on 50k fresh ones: solutions are unique, the policy only re-solves what it already solves, and the cheap samplers already sit within four points of the deployment one, so there is nothing to amortise. On K\&K a fresh-problem self-labelling loop compounds strongly ($70.5\!\to\!94.9$ over four rounds, within two points of a full-gold control), but it does so from the SFT base as well: the base already covers as many fresh problems as the RL policy, so the loop is bounded by coverage rather than by RL (Appendix~\ref{app:kk_by_n}).

